l: 111111 Status: Available  NEW ASSET: Dell Latitude 3420 Serial: 1234567 Status: Assigned Employee: Sumanth Miryala  ================================================== IMPORTANT BUSINESS LOGIC ==================================================  When Asset Replacement is completed:  1. Remove the employee assignment from the OLD asset.  2. Change OLD asset status to:    Available  3. Keep the OLD asset in the asset inventory.  4. Do NOT retire the OLD asset automatically.  5. Keep the OLD asset's:    - asset name    - serial number    - model    - category    - OS    - RAM    - location    - invoice information    - warranty information    - historical records  6. The OLD asset should be available for another employee assignment.  7. The NEW asset should be assigned to the employee.  8. The NEW asset status should be:    Assigned  9. Keep the Asset Replacement history record.  10. Keep the lifecycle/activity history showing that the replacement occurred.  ================================================== HISTORY ==================================================  The replacement history must clearly show:  OLD ASSET ↓ REPLACED BY ↓ NEW ASSET  Do NOT delete historical information.  The activity history should record the replacement normally.  For example:  Asset Replacement Employee: Sumanth Miryala Old Asset: Lenovo Thinkpad [111111] New Asset: Dell Latitude 3420 [1234567]  ================================================== INVENTORY ==================================================  After replacement, the OLD asset must appear in:  All Assets / Inventory  with:  Status: Available  It must NOT appear as:  Retired  It must be selectable for a future asset assignment.  ================================================== DO NOT CHANGE ==================================================  Do NOT change:  - Asset Replacement UI - Asset Replacement page layout - existing theme - Light Mode - Dark Mode - sidebar - active-page highlighting - Part Replacement - Employee Master - Dashboard - Authentication - Permissions - Database structure unless required - Other asset statuses - Existing replacement history  Only change the OLD ASSET status behavior during Asset Replacement.  ================================================== VALIDATION ==================================================  Make sure the following scenario works:  1. Select an employee. 2. Select the old asset. 3. Select the new asset. 4. Complete Asset Replacement. 5. Verify old asset becomes AVAILABLE. 6. Verify old asset has no current employee assignment. 7. Verify new asset becomes ASSIGNED. 8. Verify new asset is assigned to the employee. 9. Verify replacement history is created. 10. Verify activity/lifecycle history is created. 11. Verify old asset appears in Inventory as Available. 12. Verify old asset can later be assigned to another employee.  Do not simply change the UI label.  The actual backend/database business logic must use:  OLD ASSET → AVAILABLE  and not:  OLD ASSET → RETIRED  ================================================== FINAL REQUIREMENT ==================================================  Implement this cleanly in the existing application.  Inspect the existing Asset Replacement backend and frontend before changing anything.  Do not duplicate existing logic.  Do not break existing functionality.  Build and test the application from:  /home/administrator/Desktop/asset-management/  Do NOT create another workspace.  Do NOT push to Git.", 'mobile_imei': '', 'mobile_number': '48860878900', 'mobile_number_sim': '', 'model_name': 'Thinkpad ', 'network_enabled': '', 'noise_cancellation': '', 'old_device': '', 'old_user': '', 'os': '', 'os_version': '', 'printer_model': '', 'printer_type': '', 'processor': '', 'purchase_date': '', 'purchase_price': 0, 'purchase_vendor': '', 'quantity': 1, 'rack_location': '', 'raid_config': '', 'ram': '', 'refresh_rate': '', 'remarks': '', 'resolution': '', 'screen_size': '', 'serial_number': '1111111', 'size_compatibilit